// =============================================================================
// IR band map for CO₂ hydrogenation
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This file is JSONC (JSON with Comments). The build step strips // and /* */
// comments before shipping clean JSON to the browser.
//
// IMPORTANT — keep this preamble in sync: whenever a key below is added,
// removed, renamed, or its meaning changes (in schema.py, loader.py, or this
// file), update this comment block in the same change. See CLAUDE.md.
//
// Notation: every text field here is written with real Unicode characters —
// CO₂, cm⁻¹, Cu²⁺, ¹³CO, κ²-HCOO*, νₐₛ(OCO), μ₂ — and never with markup. No
// <sub>/<sup>, no <em>/<strong>, no HTML entities, no LaTeX. The chart tooltip
// renders these fields as plain text and strips any tag, while the References
// page renders tags for real, so markup makes the same sentence read
// differently in two places. build.py warns when it finds any. Underscores go
// the same way: "ν_as" is a subscript nobody typed, so write νₐₛ (or A(HF), when
// the letter has no Unicode subscript). The only underscores that belong in a
// text field are machine identifiers — band ids, group keys, enum values —
// which prose does sometimes name verbatim. The places tags ARE used in this
// project are point_group/symmetry in vibrations.jsonc and a band's own
// `symmetry` here, where the subscript is a letter Unicode has no character
// for; see vibrations.jsonc's preamble and the Notation section of the Style
// guide page.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cross-file links — the keys that actually connect the three data files.
// Everything else below is this file's own, self-contained schema.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   id:                 stable, and treated as append-only: renaming one means
//                        chasing every based_on / fermi_partner /
//                        isotopologue_of reference in THIS file, plus any
//                        description that names it verbatim. build.py catches
//                        a dangling reference, so a rename is survivable, just
//                        not free.
//   vibration_modes    — optional list of VibrationMode `id`s (from
//                        data/vibrations.jsonc) this band documents. This is
//                        the ONLY place that link is authored — a mode does
//                        NOT list its own bands; see vibrations.jsonc's own
//                        preamble for why (short version: category/subtype/
//                        atoms would otherwise have to be typed twice and
//                        kept in sync by hand). Usually 0 or 1 entries; 2
//                        only when this exact band is frequency-coincident
//                        with another mode (a real degenerate pair — see
//                        co2_bend_r below, shared by co2_bend AND
//                        co2_bend_wagging) OR when this band doesn't itself
//                        distinguish between binding geometries but the
//                        molecule's modes are split per topology (e.g.
//                        carbonate_wagging lists both
//                        carbonate_wagging_bidentate AND
//                        carbonate_wagging_monodentate — see
//                        VibrationMode.topology in vibrations.jsonc's own
//                        preamble). A combination/overtone band built from a
//                        fundamental doesn't need its own entry here at all
//                        — leave it empty and point `based_on` at the
//                        fundamental instead; loader.py chases that one level
//                        and BACK-FILLS this field at build time with the
//                        union of its based_on parents' own vibration_modes
//                        (so the emitted bands.json a combination band ships
//                        with is never actually empty when a parent links to
//                        something — this is what lets the band-chart
//                        tooltip show a combination's inherited vibration(s)
//                        too, not just a fundamental's own).
//   species            → must be a key in data/species.jsonc. This used to be
//                        a free display label; it is now the chemical
//                        identity only, and everything else the old label
//                        carried has its own field (see `phase`, `topology`,
//                        `isotope` below).
//   topology           → must be a Topology id declared by this species'
//                        molecule in data/vibrations.jsonc. build.py warns
//                        when the molecule has no such topology, so the
//                        vibration-modes page can be extended to cover it.
//   references[].measured_on, .diluent → every key must be in
//                        data/surfaces.jsonc.
//   (sets)             → the "sets" table below names selections of group
//                        keys. Nothing in a band points at a set: a set is a
//                        view over the groups, not a property of a band.
//   group              → must be a key in the "groups" table below; also
//                        what data/vibrations.jsonc's `band_groups` list
//                        points at (validated against this file's groups,
//                        not against any one band).
//   references[].key  → must resolve to a citekey in data/references.bib.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Schema v3 — one entry per band in the "bands" array:
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   id:           unique snake_case, built from what the band IS: species,
//                 mode, and whatever qualifier separates it from its siblings
//                 (branch r/p/q, topology, sym/asym, overtone). NEVER a
//                 wavenumber: the position is data, it gets refined, and an id
//                 that encodes it forces a rename every time the number moves.
//                 Where two siblings would collide, name the difference
//                 (methoxy_bend_sym / methoxy_bend_asym), do not reach for the
//                 position.
//   species:      key into data/species.jsonc, e.g. "formate". The chemical
//                 identity and nothing else. The display label and formula
//                 live in that file, so a species is spelled one way
//                 everywhere and can be validated.
//   phase:        gas | adsorbed | surface | solution | solid, or omit. Omit
//                 deliberately when the band applies to both the free molecule
//                 and its adsorbed form (several methanol fundamentals do).
//                 "surface" is for a group belonging to the solid itself, such
//                 as a support hydroxyl or a metal hydride, rather than an
//                 adsorbate. "solution" is for a species in a liquid, dissolved
//                 in it or the liquid itself: CO₂(aq), or the interfacial
//                 water an electrochemical measurement sees at the electrode.
//                 "solid" is the solid itself rather than anything on it: a
//                 lattice phonon, a framework overtone, the electronic
//                 transition of a bulk defect. Its claims have form lattice.
//                 No tag is derived from this: the phase chips come from
//                 references[].form and .medium, since nothing was measured
//                 on "a band". This field is the editorial statement of what
//                 the species is, which the sets filter on, and build.py
//                 warns where it contradicts the claims underneath it.
//   topology:     binding geometry, as a Topology id from the species'
//                 molecule in data/vibrations.jsonc: monodentate | bidentate
//                 for the oxygenates, linear | bridged | hollow | geminal |
//                 isocarbonyl for adsorbed CO. Omit for a gas-phase band, and
//                 omit when the source does not resolve the geometry.
//   symmetry:     the Mulliken label of the mode, its irreducible
//                 representation: "A<sub>1g</sub>", "E<sub>g</sub>". Written
//                 like modes[].symmetry in vibrations.jsonc, with literal
//                 <sub>/<sup> for a letter subscript, and the one band field
//                 that may carry markup (see Notation above). Usually not
//                 authored: build.py fills it from the band's own
//                 vibration_modes where those agree on one label, and never
//                 passes it on to an overtone, combination or isotopologue,
//                 whose symmetry is not its parent's. Author it where no mode
//                 carries it (a lattice phonon); build.py warns where an
//                 authored value disagrees with the modes.
//   vibration   — {category, subtype, branch}
//                 category ∈ stretch | bend | combination | lattice |
//                           electronic | rotational
//                 Two of these are not normal modes. electronic is a
//                 defect or charge-transfer transition that absorbs
//                 across the infrared. It takes atoms: "diverse".
//                 rotational is a pure rotation: nothing vibrates at
//                 all, the molecule only turns faster, and Raman
//                 reaches it directly through the polarizability. Such
//                 a band still takes a branch, the letter being only
//                 dJ; for pure rotation only dJ = +2 arises, so the
//                 branch is always S. It takes no vibration_modes,
//                 there being no normal mode to point at.
//                 subtype  ∈ symmetric | asymmetric | scissoring | wagging | rocking | twisting | null
//                 branch   ∈ O | P | Q | R | S | null
//                          The rotational branch, as ΔJ from the band
//                          centre: O = −2, P = −1, Q = 0, R = +1,
//                          S = +2.
//                          The infrared absorbs through the dipole, a
//                          rank-one operator, so it reaches ΔJ = 0, ±1
//                          and no further: P, Q, R only. An O or S band
//                          resting only on infrared claims is therefore
//                          a contradiction, and build.py warns on it.
//                          Raman scatters through the polarizability, a
//                          rank-two tensor, so it reaches ΔJ up to ±2.
//                          WHICH of the five it shows depends on the
//                          rotor: a linear molecule in a non-degenerate
//                          vibration gets O, Q, S only (N₂, H₂), while
//                          a symmetric top, a spherical top, or a linear
//                          molecule in a degenerate vibration gets all
//                          five. So P and R on a Raman-only band are NOT
//                          an error in general: methane’s F₂ band
//                          genuinely has them.
//                 j        ∈ integer ⩾ 0, or null
//                          The rotational level the transition STARTS
//                          from, set only when the source resolves one
//                          line rather than a branch envelope. With it
//                          the band is labelled S(3) in the
//                          spectroscopist’s form; without it the label
//                          is the bare branch letter and the band is the
//                          whole envelope. Use it where the lines are
//                          genuinely apart, as they are for H₂ whose
//                          rotational constant is about 59 cm⁻¹, and
//                          leave it out where they blur into a tail, as
//                          for N₂ at about 2 cm⁻¹. Requires branch.
//                 note: overtones use the parent's category + tags: ["overtone"]
//   atoms       — which atoms/bond actually move in this band, e.g. "O=C=O",
//                 "C-H"; "diverse" for a combination band whose two parent
//                 modes involve unrelated atom groups, and for an
//                 electronic band, where no atom moves. Drives the "atoms"
//                 color dimension in the chart and the atoms chip in the
//                 vibration-modes page.
//   wn_start, wn_end — the band's wavenumber range (cm⁻¹) as drawn on the
//                 chart. Often an envelope (e.g. an R/P branch spread) rather
//                 than one precise line — see references[].wn below for a
//                 specific source's own exact value.
//   short       — short display label for the chart/legend, e.g.
//                 "νₐₛ(OCO) CO₂ (R)". Falls back to an auto-generated label
//                 (species + vibration) if omitted.
//   description — a band-level synthesis, independent of any single
//                 reference's wording — this is the editorial summary, not a
//                 quote from one paper. Should say more than just restating
//                 vibration.category/subtype: what makes this band worth
//                 recognizing experimentally (what it overlaps with or gets
//                 confused for, whether it's a diagnostic/marker band for a
//                 species or a mechanistic step, pressure/temperature
//                 behavior, why a symmetry-forbidden transition still gets an
//                 entry). A specific paper's specific claim belongs in that
//                 reference's own `note` instead (see references[].note) —
//                 not here.
//   based_on    — list of parent-mode references, each with `multiplier` plus
//                 exactly one of:
//                   band_id      — one specific observed band (the common
//                                  case: the parent vibration has no
//                                  resolved R/P/Q branching)
//                   branch_group — the parent *vibration* via its
//                                  branch_group key instead of one specific
//                                  branch, used when the parent is itself
//                                  branched and the composition doesn't
//                                  depend on which branch was observed
//                   (neither, + label) — parent mode outside the dataset
//                                  entirely, e.g. IR-inactive ν₁ of CO₂
//   references  — list of citation entries, resolved against references.bib.
//                 Each entry is either a bare BibTeX key string (shorthand for
//                 {key}), or an object {id, key, wn, measured_on, diluent,
//                 technique, note, tags}. key is mandatory: entries without one are
//                 disregarded.
//                   id   — the claim's own number, a positive integer,
//                          unique across the whole file. A new claim takes
//                          metadata.next_claim_id and that mark goes up
//                          by one; it never goes down, so a deleted claim's
//                          number is never handed out again. Editing a claim
//                          (its wn, site, note, even its technique) keeps its
//                          number. One band can hold several claims from the
//                          same paper (a second site, a second position), and
//                          each has its own. build.py fails on a missing,
//                          repeated or out-of-range id and names the next free
//                          ones.
//                   wn   — THIS source's own reported wavenumber, when it
//                          differs from the band's general wn_start/wn_end
//                          range (sources often disagree by a few cm⁻¹). Use
//                          a JSON array when this one source resolves
//                          multiple distinct components under this band
//                          (e.g. separate p-/s-polarized peaks) — same
//                          array-or-scalar convention as site.
//                   measured_on: where this source measured the band, as one
//                          or more keys from data/surfaces.jsonc. Entries
//                          there carry a `level`: a site (cu_1p) is the
//                          atom-scale spot, a material any piece of solid,
//                          one compound (tio2, al2o3_gamma) or a whole
//                          catalyst (cu_zno). Name the catalyst measured AND
//                          whatever the paper placed the species on: a site,
//                          a support, or both. ["cu_1p", "cu_zno"] reads "on
//                          Cu⁺ of Cu/ZnO"; ["tio2", "pt_moox_tio2"] "on the
//                          titania of that catalyst". One that names only the
//                          catalyst gets one key, and that is a complete
//                          record of what it said: seen there, not located.
//                          Name the most specific record the paper supports
//                          (γ-Al₂O₃, not Al₂O₃): a search for the parent still
//                          finds it. One measurement on one solid per row: two
//                          catalysts compared are two claims, and build.py
//                          warns where a row names two specimens. No reaction
//                          conditions here: those belong in note.
//                   diluent: keys from data/surfaces.jsonc for what the
//                          specimen was mixed with but is not part of it
//                          (KBr in a DRIFTS cup, an α-Al₂O₃ bed).
//                          Kept apart from measured_on so it never enters
//                          the site description or the element sets.
//                   specimens, placement, site_elements, specimen_elements:
//                          derived by build.py, never authored. specimens is
//                          what was investigated: the materials named that
//                          no other named material contains, so the
//                          hierarchy of the keys decides the role (TiO₂ alone
//                          is the specimen, TiO₂ beside Pt/TiO₂ a part).
//                          placement is how far the claim places the
//                          species: site (a site is named), material (a part
//                          of the specimen is named, or the specimen is one
//                          compound) or specimen (only a composite is named:
//                          seen there, location not stated). site_elements
//                          is the union of the elements of that placement
//                          (what the molecule could be bound to),
//                          specimen_elements of every key named (what was
//                          anywhere in the specimen). Diluents count in
//                          neither.
//                   technique: how the spectrum was taken: a key of
//                          data/techniques.jsonc (drifts, transmission, atr,
//                          seiras, irras, pm_irras, emission, mioirs, infrared,
//                          raman, srs, computational), where each one's label,
//                          family, parent and note live. build.py fails on any
//                          other value. The
//                          infrared values
//                          name a sampling geometry, with one exception:
//                          mioirs (Mixed Isotope Operando IR) names what was
//                          fed to the cell, a mixed isotopic stream that
//                          detunes the dipole coupling between neighbouring
//                          adsorbates, because a decoupled adlayer is a
//                          different measurement whichever geometry recorded
//                          it. seiras is surface-enhanced IR on a metal-film
//                          electrode, nearly always in the ATR (Kretschmann)
//                          geometry; it keeps a value apart from atr because
//                          the surface selection rule applies and the spectra
//                          are differences against a reference potential, so
//                          a band can point down for a species consumed.
//                          infrared is the placeholder
//                          for a source that says IR (or FTIR) and names no
//                          geometry, the way raman is for a source that says
//                          only Raman. raman is a Raman shift, whatever the
//                          geometry. The chart's Techniques filter decides
//                          which claims count; a claim with no technique
//                          never does. Leave the field out only when
//                          the paper says nothing at all.
//                          build.py derives a tag of the same name, so never
//                          write one by hand.
//                   medium, form: how the sample was held for THIS claim,
//                          on two axes, because one word cannot say both.
//                          medium is what surrounded it: vacuum (an
//                          evacuated or UHV cell, no gas let in) | gas (any
//                          gas atmosphere, a fraction of a mbar of dosed CO
//                          included) | liquid (a liquid or an electrolyte) |
//                          supercritical (a dense fluid above its critical
//                          point) | matrix (a frozen noble gas). A
//                          calculation records what its model contains: a
//                          slab or cluster in vacuum, unless it adds a solvent. form is where the
//                          species was: free (on its own: a gas, the liquid
//                          itself, matrix-isolated) | dissolved (a solute) |
//                          adsorbed (on a surface) | surface (a group of the
//                          solid: a hydroxyl, a hydride) | lattice (a mode or
//                          a defect of the solid itself). CO adsorbed in UHV,
//                          under a gas and at an electrode is then three
//                          different rows, as it is three different numbers.
//                          Not the band's own `phase` above: that says what
//                          the band is wherever it appears. Leave medium out
//                          where the paper does not say. Each derives a tag
//                          ("in liquid", "adsorbed"), so never write one by
//                          hand.
//                   laser_nm: the Raman excitation wavelength in nm, as a
//                          plain number ("laser_nm": 515). Only for a
//                          claim whose technique is raman; build.py warns
//                          otherwise, since an infrared measurement has no
//                          excitation line. It derives a chip of its own,
//                          "515 nm", drawn in the colour of that light
//                          rather than from a fixed table, because a
//                          wavelength is a number and no tag vocabulary
//                          could hold it. Record it only where the paper
//                          names the line: "argon ion laser" with no
//                          wavelength is not one.
//                   scattering: stokes | anti-stokes, which side of the
//                          laser line a Raman claim was read on. Both are
//                          the same mode at the same distance from the line,
//                          so an anti-Stokes line stays a claim on the same
//                          band and wn is the magnitude: a paper's −418 is
//                          "wn": 418, "scattering": "anti-stokes". An
//                          anti-Stokes claim only where the source prints an
//                          anti-Stokes position; a paper that merely says it
//                          saw the anti-Stokes lines says so in the note of
//                          the Stokes claim. Leave it out on a plain positive
//                          shift: build.py fills in "stokes" on every Raman
//                          claim that names no side, so every Raman claim
//                          carries exactly one of the two tags. Raman only
//                          (build.py warns otherwise). Derives a tag of the
//                          same name. A band with a counted anti-Stokes claim
//                          is also drawn at the negative shift on the chart.
//                   note — THIS source's specifics: experimental conditions
//                          (catalyst, loading, temperature, pressure,
//                          pretreatment) and any claim/finding unique to that
//                          one paper (e.g. "confirmed as RWGS intermediate by
//                          ¹³C isotopic exchange at 200°C"). Contrast with the
//                          band-level `description` above, which stays true
//                          independent of which source you're reading.
//                   tags — scoped to this one citation, not the whole band
//                          (e.g. only this source's claim is a
//                          "misassignment-warning"); styled the same as
//                          band-level tags in the frontend. "isotope-
//                          labeling" marks a source that used isotope
//                          substitution as EVIDENCE for the attribution — a
//                          different claim from the band-level
//                          "isotope", which says the band itself IS
//                          an isotopologue and is only ever auto-added via
//                          isotopologue_of above; never write
//                          "isotope" as a reference tag.
//                          "direct-dosing"
//                          marks a reference where the surface species was
//                          generated by dosing/adsorbing its own desorbed
//                          counterpart directly (e.g. formate from formic
//                          acid, methoxy from methanol), not produced via a
//                          genuine surface reaction (e.g. from CO₂+H₂).
//                          It is a statement about where the species came
//                          from, independent of the technique, so it sits
//                          on a transmission claim and a DRIFTS one alike;
//                          it only means anything for a surface species,
//                          since a gas in a cell was dosed by definition.
//                          Not a criticism either: dosing is how a clean
//                          reference spectrum gets made. It says the band
//                          is evidence for that species, and not evidence
//                          that the species forms under reaction.
//                 Example: {"key": "Smith.Example.2020", "wn": 1350,
//                           "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cu_zno"],
//                           "technique": "drifts", "note": "...",
//                           "tags": ["..."]}
//                 Multi-wn:   {"key": "...", "wn": [1555, 1548]}
//   tags        — free-form list of strings, band-level (as opposed to the
//                 per-citation tags above, which are scoped to one
//                 reference); "overtone" marks overtone bands.
//                 Auto-added, never written by hand: "isotope" (from
//                 isotopologue_of), "fermi-resonance" (from fermi_partner),
//                 "rotational-branches" (from branch_group)
//                 and "fundamental" (from vibration.category
//                 plus based_on: anything that is not a combination, an
//                 overtone, built on another mode, or electronic).
//   fermi_partner — id of the other band in a Fermi resonance doublet, or
//                 omit/null. Must be reciprocal (A.fermi_partner=B AND
//                 B.fermi_partner=A) for the pair to be considered complete;
//                 build.py then auto-adds "fermi-resonance" to both bands'
//                 tags. A one-sided link raises a build warning, not an error.
//   fermi_partner_group — like fermi_partner, but pointing at an entire
//                 branch_group instead of one band, used when the resonance
//                 partner vibration is itself split into branches (e.g. two
//                 multi-branch combination bands resonating with each
//                 other). Mutually exclusive with fermi_partner.
//   isotopologue_of — id of the band this one is the isotope-substituted
//                 twin of: the SAME normal mode, measured on a heavier
//                 molecule (e.g. ν(C-D) of κ²-DCOO* -> ν(C-H) of
//                 κ²-HCOO*). Deliberately ONE-directional, child -> parent,
//                 unlike the reciprocal fermi_partner: only the substituted
//                 band carries the link and only it gets the auto
//                 "isotope" tag (added by loader.tag_isotopologues),
//                 because the parent's position is a property of the
//                 ordinary molecule and shouldn't be relabelled just
//                 because someone measured its heavy twin. No chains — the
//                 parent must itself be a natural-abundance band. Like a
//                 combination's based_on, this link back-fills
//                 vibration_modes: leave that empty on an isotopologue and
//                 the loader inherits the parent's.
//                 A source's isotopologue value belongs in a band of its
//                 own via this field, NOT crammed into the natural-abundance
//                 band's references[].wn array.
//   isotope     — which substitution, as display text: "D", "¹³C", "¹⁸O".
//                 Required exactly when isotopologue_of is set (and
//                 forbidden otherwise).
//   branch_group — shared string key linking the R/P/Q branches of one
//                 vibrational transition (2-way for R/P, 3-way when Q is
//                 also IR-allowed), or omit/null. All bands sharing the same
//                 key become mutual partners; build.py auto-adds
//                 "rotational-branches" to all of them. A key used by only
//                 one band raises a build warning (likely a typo or a
//                 forgotten sibling), not an error.
//   vibration_modes — see "Cross-file links" above. Omit/[] if this band
//                 isn't documented on the vibration-modes page.
//   intensity   — signal strength: vs | s | m | w | vw  (omit if unknown)
//   width       — spectral linewidth: sharp | medium | broad | very_broad  (omit if unknown)
//   confidence  — how firmly the band is identified: confirmed | likely | tentative | speculative  (omit if unknown)
//   lane, sub_lane — NOT set in this file. Computed at load time by
//                 layout.py from the "lanes" table above (which row this
//                 band's group sits in) plus the overlap stagger, and added
//                 to the emitted JSON for the frontend.
// =============================================================================
{
  "metadata": {
    "title": "IR band map for CO₂ hydrogenation",
    "wavenumber_unit": "cm^-1",
    "description": "Approximate band positions for surface and gas-phase species in CO₂ hydrogenation. Exact positions depend on support, coverage, and reaction conditions. The asterisk (*) denotes a surface-bound species.",
    "schema_version": 3,
    // The next free claim id. Every references[] entry carries its own
    // number (see the preamble); a new claim takes this one and the mark goes
    // up by one. It never goes down, so a deleted claim's number is never
    // handed out again.
    "next_claim_id": 559
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Spectral regions — used for layout grouping and the lane-label margin.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "regions": {
    "very_high": {
      "label": "Very high frequency",
      "wn_min": 4000,
      "wn_max": 5000
    },
    "high": {
      "label": "High frequency",
      "wn_min": 2500,
      "wn_max": 4000
    },
    "mid_hi": {
      "label": "Mid-high (gas/CO)",
      "wn_min": 2000,
      "wn_max": 2500
    },
    "mid": {
      "label": "Mid (fingerprint)",
      "wn_min": 1200,
      "wn_max": 2000
    },
    "low": {
      "label": "Low frequency",
      "wn_min": 500,
      "wn_max": 1200
    },
    "very_low": {
      "label": "Very low (lattice)",
      "wn_min": 100,
      "wn_max": 500
    }
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Chemical groups: the default colour scheme and the lane labels.
  // The color is the 'family color'; bands inherit it via b.group.
  // Since 2026-10-18 the species groups take their colours from the phd_ui
  // species scheme (phd_ui.colors), so a molecule has one colour in the atlas,
  // the DRIFTS figures and the MS traces: gas CO2 dark blue, CO amber, CH4
  // red, H2 blue, H2O ocean; the target family green (methoxy the darker
  // tone, methanol the line tone); formate pink, the carbonates the scheme's
  // two browns, hydride purple. CO* variants are the amber ladder. The
  // specimen lanes keep their own muted colours.
  // `of` says what vibrates. "species" (the default, left out) is a molecule
  // or an adsorbate, a hydride included; "specimen" is the solid itself: its
  // hydroxyls and its lattice. The chart draws every specimen lane below every
  // species lane, under a divider, and build.py fails on a lanes table that
  // puts one above the other.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "groups": {
    "support_oh": {
      "label": "Support OH",
      "color": "#3D7E78",
      "of": "specimen"
    },
    "water": {
      "label": "Water",
      "color": "#0077BE"
    },
    "formate": {
      "label": "Formate*",
      "color": "#C93F76"
    },
    "carbonyl": {
      "label": "Carbonyl*",
      "color": "#7D6128"
    },
    "carbonate": {
      "label": "Carbonate*",
      "color": "#5A1B00"
    },
    "bicarbonate": {
      "label": "Bicarbonate*",
      "color": "#827359"
    },
    "methoxy": {
