      "description": "CO bonded end-on to a single metal atom, the commonest coordination and usually the strongest, sharpest band in a CO-probe spectrum. Its position follows metal to CO π-backdonation: more backdonation into the 2π* orbital weakens the C-O bond and pushes the band down, while electron-poor or oxidised sites push it up, so metal identity, oxidation state, coordination and coverage all move it. ¹³CO substitution shifts it by tens of cm⁻¹, the standard check that a band really is a CO stretch. Coverage moves it as far as the site does, partly by stiffening the bond and partly by coupling neighbouring oscillators, so the observed bands are collective modes of the adlayer rather than one band per kind of CO.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 51,
          "key": "Proano.InsituDRIFTSStudy.2019",
