          "note": "Fig. 4a (spatially resolved SSITKA-DRIFTS, 3 wt% Pt/CeO₂ reduced in 10% H₂ at 300 °C; 200 °C, 1 bar, H₂/CO₂ = 4 in He): linear ¹²CO on Pt at 2053 cm⁻¹, red-shifted from the usual 2070–2080 cm⁻¹ of CO on Pt. The paper reads the shift as stronger back-donation from Pt, because the highly reduced ceria surface hinders electron transfer from Pt to the support, and takes it to place the carbonyls at the Pt-ceria interface. After the ¹³CO₂ switch the carbonyl exchanges at the pace of CO(g) at every bed position (Fig. 5) and is most abundant at the inlet (Fig. 6), the paper's case for it as an RWGS intermediate rather than readsorbed product. The ¹³C counterpart is its own band, ν(¹³CO) linear (μ₁).",
