          "technique": "mioirs",
          "tags": [
            "isotope-labeling"
          ],
          "note": "Section 3 of a perspective, on the isotope-dilution method rather than on one spectrum, which is why no position is recorded here; the technique is the one the perspective proposes, MIOIRS. The ¹³CO and ¹²CO singletons lie about 50 cm⁻¹ apart, enough to cut the coupling between a molecule and its neighbours by roughly an order of magnitude once one is diluted in the other; ¹³C¹⁸O against ¹²C¹⁶O gives 100 cm⁻¹ and cuts it further. Diluting at a fixed coverage separates the chemical and coupling halves of a coverage shift, and recovers bands that intensity transfer had hidden, such as CO on copper terraces. The perspective proposes running it under reaction conditions and names that MIOIRS. No measurement of its own; gas is recorded because the method is proposed for catalysts under reaction, this collection’s reading."
        }
      ],
