          "id": 68,
          "key": "Elgayyar.ContributionsLimitationsIR.2021",
          "wn": [
            1987,
            1954
          ],
          "measured_on": [
            "pd_0",
            "pd_al2o3"
          ],
          "technique": "infrared",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "Fig. 6A, p. 63 (2 % Pd/Al₂O₃, 1 % CO in He at 300 K, adapted from Jbir et al.; the review says IR and not which sampling geometry): two bridged carbonyls at 1987 and 1954 cm⁻¹ beside atop CO at about 2090. On a Pd-Sn sample of equal Pd and Sn loading only the atop species survives, at about 2075, and Zn does the same (Fig. 7A). That disappearance is the review's evidence for an intermetallic compound: bridged CO needs two adjacent palladium atoms, so its absence says the pairs are gone. Adsorption heats separate the two cleanly, about 90 kJ mol⁻¹ for the atop CO against more than 165 for the bridged."
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "key": "Monai.NewLookCatalyst.2024",
          "medium": "vacuum",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": [
            1895,
            1997
          ],
          "measured_on": [
            "pd_100"
          ],
          "technique": "irras",
          "tags": [
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "Section 2.2 and Fig. 3A of a perspective, reviewing RAIRS of CO on the Pd(100) single crystal (ref. 46, 1982): a single band moving from 1895 to 1997 cm⁻¹ as the coverage goes from about zero to 0.8, almost 100 cm⁻¹ in all, of which roughly 60 is a chemical shift and the rest dipole coupling. The perspective labels the band atop CO; the positions are the bridged range for palladium, and it is recorded on the bridged band here for that reason. The integrated area levels off near half a monolayer, so area stops tracking coverage well before saturation. Single-crystal RAIRS is a vacuum measurement; the perspective does not state the pressure, so vacuum is this collection’s assumption."
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "key": "Davydov.MolecularSpectroscopyOxide.2003",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Section 2.3.2, p. 96 of a monograph, not one measurement: bridged carbonyls lie below 1900 cm⁻¹ and three-coordinated ones below 1800. The book adds a constraint the position alone does not give: a carbonyl on a cation can only be linear, because in an M–O–M arrangement the cations are too far apart for one CO molecule to bond to two of them at once. A bridged band is therefore evidence of an ensemble of reduced metal atoms rather than of a cation, whatever else is on the surface. Where a cation has two free coordination places it forms a dicarbonyl instead. A summary rather than one measurement; the experiments it draws on adsorb CO on samples held under the probe gas, so gas is this collection’s reading."
        },
        {
          "id": 394,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "wn": [
            1848,
            1774
          ],
          "measured_on": [
            "ag_au_film"
          ],
          "technique": "seiras",
          "medium": "liquid",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "Figs. S16 and S17: Ag film electrode in CO-saturated 0.1 M Cs₂CO₃ (SI caption; the main text says CsHCO₃), swept from +0.7 to −1.1 V vs. RHE. Below about −0.35 V bridged CO takes over from linear CO, between 1848 and 1774 cm⁻¹, with a Stark tuning slope of 85.8 cm⁻¹ V⁻¹, steeper than the 57.7 of linear CO; its integrated intensity peaks near −0.7 V. The two numbers are the ends of the sweep. They lie low for a bridge because the potential is strongly cathodic."
        },
        {
          "id": 395,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "wn": [
            1760,
            1757,
            1765
          ],
          "measured_on": [
            "ag_au_film"
          ],
          "technique": "seiras",
          "medium": "liquid",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "note": "Fig. 2a, e, f: Ag film in CO₂-saturated 0.1 M CsHCO₃. Under static electrolysis at −1.1 V vs. RHE bridged CO is the only CO species, at 1760 cm⁻¹, its intensity levelling off after about 8 s. Deconvolved: 1757 under static conditions (Fig. 2e, 188.3 s) and 1765 under +0.7 V pulses (Fig. 2f, 526.7 s); the pulsed spectra of Fig. 2b label it across 1857 to 1760. It trades intensity with linear CO at every potential switch. The bridge attribution rests on the CO-dosed sweep and on a calculation on Ag(111)."
        },
        {
          "id": 403,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "medium": "vacuum",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1867,
          "measured_on": [
            "ag_111"
