      "short": "ν(CO) 3-fold (μ₃)",
      "description": "Three-fold hollow CO coordinated to three metal atoms at once. Stronger multi-center π-backdonation shifts ν(CO) well below both bridge and linear adsorption. Generally needs a close-packed facet to offer this coordination geometry at all, and tends to appear or strengthen with increasing coverage as simpler one- and two-fold sites fill up first.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 71,
          "key": "Yudanov.COAdsorptionPd.2026",
          "medium": "vacuum",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1810,
          "measured_on": "pd_0",
          "technique": "computational",
          "tags": [
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "DFT-calculated (scaled) frequencies of 3-fold hollow CO on Pd(111)-type facets/nanoparticles range from ~1802–1815 cm⁻¹ at low coverage up to ~1902 cm⁻¹ at the high-coverage (2×2)-3CO structure (0.75 ML), tracking the same coverage-dependent blue shift seen for bridge-bound CO. Calculated on Pd nanoparticle models in vacuum."
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "key": "Elgayyar.ContributionsLimitationsIR.2021",
          "wn": 1850,
          "measured_on": [
            "co_0",
            "co_al2o3"
          ],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Figs. 8A and 9A, p. 65 (14 % Co/Al₂O₃, operando DRIFTS at 220 °C, 30 % CO + 60 % H₂ in Ar, atmospheric pressure, 22 h on stream): a broad band near 1850 cm⁻¹ the review calls Hollow-CO and describes as bridged or multi-coordinated without separating the two, sitting under two linear components between 2100 and 1950. Small amounts of tin, Sn:Co from 1:120 to 1:30, titrate it selectively, and the methane and propene rates track the Hollow-CO fraction. Its decomposition rate is about twice the rate of methane formation, which the review takes as evidence that it is on the main path to hydrocarbons while the linear CO is not."
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "key": "Davydov.MolecularSpectroscopyOxide.2003",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Section 2.3.2, p. 96 of a monograph, not one measurement: three-coordinated bridged carbonyls lie below 1800 cm⁻¹, the lowest of the three coordination ranges the book sets out, because the more metal atoms share the CO the more they donate into its 2π* and the weaker the C–O bond becomes. A summary rather than one measurement; the experiments it draws on adsorb CO on samples held under the probe gas, so gas is this collection’s reading."
        },
        {
          "id": 396,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "wn": [
