          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2125,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Fig. 3a (KRS-5 transmission cell, −160 °C, 0.15 mbar CO, static vacuum): a Cu⁺-CO feature absent from the unpromoted Cu/SiO₂ and appearing only once Ga is loaded. Its deconvoluted area gives the Cu⁺/(Cu⁰+Cu⁺) ratio, which rises linearly with Ga content (Fig. S4a) and correlates with TOF(MeOH) at every temperature with R² above 0.95 (Fig. 3b). Assigned to Cu⁺ at the Cu-Ga₂O₃ interface, the proposed methanol active site.",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2147,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Figs. S3a and S3b (DRIFTS, 5% CO/He flowing at 20 mL min⁻¹, 1 bar): the same Cu⁺ carbonyl at high coverage under flowing CO, 22 cm⁻¹ above the value the transmission experiment gives at −160 °C under vacuum. The paper attributes the whole difference to coverage and to static against dynamic conditions rather than to a different site, noting that the Cu⁰ to Cu⁺ separation holds near 27 cm⁻¹ in both. Well defined at 220 °C only on CuGa(0.15) and CuGa(0.20); desorption is followed in Figs. S5 and S6.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2196,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Figs. S3c, S4b and S10 (DRIFTS, 10% CO₂/Ar at 20 mL min⁻¹, 3 bar, 220 °C): no CO is fed here. The band grows when CO₂ alone is dosed, and the authors read it as Cu⁺(O)-CO, the carbonyl left on a Cu⁺ that has just split CO₂ into CO* and O*. It is absent on the unpromoted catalyst and its area scales with Ga loading (Fig. S4b), which is their evidence that the interfacial Cu⁺ dissociates CO₂ without hydrogen. Switching to H₂/Ar moves it to 2165 cm⁻¹ (Fig. S10), read as the oxygen being taken off the site.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"]
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Gomez.InterdependenceExtentGa.2024",
          "wn": 2165,
          "measured_on": ["cu_1p", "cuga_sio2"],
          "note": "Table S5 and Fig. S10 (operando DRIFTS, 220 °C, H₂/CO₂ = 3, 6 bar, 25 mL min⁻¹): Cu⁺-CO under working conditions, and the band the 2196 cm⁻¹ Cu⁺(O)-CO turns into once H₂ reaches the surface. This is the carbonyl the paper's RWGS plus CO-hydrogenation route runs through, so its presence under reaction rather than under a CO probe is the point of the measurement. Literature values quoted alongside it are 2166 to 2173 cm⁻¹.",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": []
