          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 5A, p. 63 (Au, Au-Ag and Ag embedded in silicalite-1, operando DRIFTS at 100 °C under 2.8 % CO + 8 % O₂ in He): Ag⁺-CO at 2189 and 2172 cm⁻¹ on the plain silver sample moves to about 2160 on the alloy, and the Au⁺-CO signal at 2134 falls by half with the gold loading and particle size unchanged. The two together are the review's evidence for a genuinely alloyed phase. Silver has the lower surface tension of the pair, 1086 against 1333 mJ m⁻², so it takes the corner and edge sites and displaces the gold that would otherwise sit there."
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "key": "Davydov.MolecularSpectroscopyOxide.2003",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 2190,
          "measured_on": [
            "zn_2p",
            "zno"
          ],
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Section 2.3.2, pp. 95–96 and Table 2.11, p. 99 (a monograph; the sampling geometry behind the number is not stated). One band at 2190 cm⁻¹ after CO adsorption on ZnO at 293 K, with photoelectron work on the same system showing the bond is made through the carbon and the 5σ orbital. On a coordinatively unsaturated cation the bond is essentially σ only, so ν(CO) sits above the 2143 cm⁻¹ of the free molecule, and the shift upward grows with the cation's ratio of charge to radius. Such complexes are relatively unstable and are usually removed by degassing at room temperature, which is one way to tell them from the far more stable M⁺ and M⁰ carbonyls (p. 274). Measured under CO: the complex is removed by degassing at room temperature."
        }
