      "description": "Bicarbonate asymmetric OCO stretch, the strongest band of the species together with its symmetric partner. Its splitting from that partner, around 210 to 220 cm⁻¹, is the geometry diagnostic: adsorbed bicarbonate bridges two neighbouring metal sites through both of its non-protonated oxygens rather than binding through one, and the splitting comes out the same on oxides as different as iron and aluminium. Distinguishing it from carbonate rests on the COH bend near 1220 cm⁻¹ and on its disappearance under deuteration, since carbonate has no proton to lose.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 140,
          "key": "Proano.InsituDRIFTSStudy.2019",
          "wn": 1650,
          "measured_on": ["al2o3_gamma", "ru_al2o3"],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "note": "Fig. 1a and Section 3.1 (5%Ru/Al₂O₃, 320 °C, 15% CO₂/N₂, Harrick cell with ZnSe windows, background the same material under N₂ at 320 °C): the O-C-O stretch of bicarbonate, paired with 1440 cm⁻¹. The same pair appears on bare γ-Al₂O₃ (Fig. 1b), which is the paper's argument that the alumina hydroxyls alone make bicarbonate from CO₂ without any Ru.",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "id": 141,
          "key": "Rajput.InsituDRIFTSKineticthermodynamic.2025",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1650,
          "measured_on": "al2o3_gamma",
          "note": "Table 3: δ(–OH) at 1650 cm⁻¹; formed by CO₂ interaction with surface OH groups; suppressed by La modification and Ni-Ce-Fe loading."
        },
        {
          "id": 142,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Baltrusaitis.FTIRSpectroscopyCombined.2006",
          "wn": 1622,
          "measured_on": "fe2o3",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling", "direct-dosing"],
          "note": "Table 2, ν₂(OCO)ₐ. Nanoparticulate amorphous Fe₂O₃, 229 m² g⁻¹, hydroxylated, 274 mTorr CO₂ at 296 K and RH below 1%. One of the three strongest bicarbonate bands, with the symmetric partner at 1410 and the COH bend at 1220 cm⁻¹. It sits 27 cm⁻¹ below the same mode on the aluminas, the largest difference between the oxides in this study. Substituting D and ¹⁸O and comparing all five isotopologues against five DFT cluster models is what identifies the structure as bridged rather than monodentate. Table 3 gives the ¹⁸O positions of this mode: 1599 cm⁻¹ for HC¹⁸O₃⁻ and 1613 and 1609 cm⁻¹ for the two mixed isotopologues."
        },
        {
          "id": 143,
          "key": "Baltrusaitis.FTIRSpectroscopyCombined.2006",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1650,
          "measured_on": [
            "al2o3_alpha"
          ],
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": [
            "isotope-labeling",
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "Table 2, ν₂(OCO)ₐ of bicarbonate on hydroxylated α-Al₂O₃ (Alfa Aesar, 12.4 m² g⁻¹), 274 mTorr CO₂ at 296 K: 1650 cm⁻¹, against 1648 on γ-Al₂O₃ in the same table. The paper averages the two aluminas for its comparison with Fe₂O₃, finding they differ by a few wavenumbers at most."
        },
        {
          "id": 144,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "wn": 1633,
          "measured_on": "ga2o3",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"],
          "note": "CO₂ dosed on precipitated Ga₂O₃ at 300 °C (Fig. S12): of the three bands formed, this one is assigned to bicarbonate and the other two to carbonate. It is the least stable of them: Ar purging removes the bicarbonate while the carbonates stay, which is the paper's evidence that the carbonate, not the bicarbonate, is what the surface hydride goes on to reduce. Table S2 names the mode δ(OH) of CO₃H at 1634 cm⁻¹ rather than the OCO stretch this band records, the same label Rajput et al. use at 1650 cm⁻¹; the species is not in dispute, only which motion carries the band."
        },
        {
          "id": 145,
          "key": "Oing.RoleZnHf.2026",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1622,
          "measured_on": [
            "znhfox"
          ],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": [
            "direct-dosing"
          ],
          "note": "Figs. 6a, S56d and S56e (operando DRIFTS, 320 °C, 20 bar, 20 vol% CO₂ in N₂ for 1 h, background N₂ at 320 °C): bidentate bicarbonate, b-CO₃H*, at 1622 cm⁻¹ on 30ZnHfOₓ, 1603 cm⁻¹ on 20ZnHfOₓ and 1604 cm⁻¹ on 90ZnHfOₓ. The paper labels the species and not the mode; this band is where the position falls. It disappears instantly under H₂ together with the carbonate. The carbonate-to-bicarbonate intensity ratio is similar on 20 and 90ZnHfOₓ, the SI's evidence that CO₂ activation does not change when the bulk becomes ZnO. The window is widened to 1600 cm⁻¹ for the two lower values."
        },
        {
