    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // SURFACE HYDROXYLS
    // Isolated (sharp, high) vs H-bonded (broad, lower) — support-dependent positions.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
      "id": "oh_surface",
      "species": "hydroxyl",
      "phase": "surface",
      "group": "support_oh",
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "subtype": null,
        "branch": null
      },
      "atoms": "O-H",
      "wn_start": 3400,
      "wn_end": 3750,
      "short": "ν(OH) surface",
      "description": "Surface hydroxyl stretch on an oxide: the O-H left behind wherever water or H₂ dissociates on it. The window is wide because the position tracks the coordination of the cation carrying the group and how strongly it is hydrogen-bonded. Discrete sharp components above roughly 3600 cm⁻¹ come from terminal hydroxyls on distinct cation environments, and hydrogen bonding drags them down and merges them into a broad envelope. Counting the sharp components is how the kinds of cation site a surface exposes get counted. The region is normally read as a difference spectrum, since adsorbates consume hydroxyls and push the band negative.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 330,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "wn": 3685,
          "measured_on": ["ga_tet", "ga2o3"],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 1 (precipitated Ga₂O₃, 0.1 MPa H₂ at 450 °C): the higher of two hydroxyls resolved on one surface, assigned to the OH on a tetrahedrally coordinated gallium and formed together with the Ga-H at 2011 cm⁻¹. The split from the other hydroxyl follows the two gallium coordinations, not hydrogen bonding. Both O-H bands are about five times weaker than the hydride bands they accompany, the opposite of the usual intensity order, which the authors read as evidence that hydride rather than hydroxyl is what accumulates here."
        },
        {
          "id": 331,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "wn": 3532,
          "measured_on": ["ga_oct", "ga2o3"],
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 1 (precipitated Ga₂O₃, 0.1 MPa H₂ at 450 °C): the lower of the two hydroxyls, on an octahedrally coordinated gallium, formed together with the Ga-H at 1988 cm⁻¹. Two claims rather than one array, because the paper assigns each position to its own gallium site."
        },
        {
          "id": 332,
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "wn": 3450,
          "measured_on": "ga2o3",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. S4 (as-prepared precipitated Ga₂O₃, captioned “FTIR spectrum of Ga₂O₃ sample referenced to KBr”, so a transmission measurement, unlike the in-situ work of the paper, which is DRIFTS in a Harrick chamber reported in Kubelka-Munk units): a broad hydroxyl envelope centred near 3450 cm⁻¹, with an OH bend marked near 1640 cm⁻¹. This is the oxide before any H₂ treatment, and the paper uses it to argue that the surface already carries a substantial hydroxyl coverage, so the sharp 3685 and 3532 cm⁻¹ pair grown under H₂ at 450 °C is a small addition to an already hydroxylated surface. Both numbers are approximate in the figure's own labels. The paper does not state the atmosphere; an ex-situ spectrum of the as-prepared powder is taken in air, which is this collection’s assumption."
        },
        {
          "id": 333,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "key": "Baltrusaitis.FTIRSpectroscopyCombined.2006",
          "wn": [3686, 3664],
          "measured_on": "fe2o3",
          "technique": "transmission",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"],
          "note": "Fig. 2 (hydroxylated nanoparticulate Fe₂O₃, 274 mTorr CO₂ at 296 K): both bands appear as negative peaks, since CO₂ consumes these hydroxyls to make bicarbonate. They are assigned to OH bonded to two and to three iron atoms respectively, with singly coordinated hydroxyls expected near 3700 cm⁻¹ and more highly coordinated ones lower. A broad band near 3300 cm⁻¹ is the hydrogen-bonded population."
        },
        {
          "id": 334,
          "medium": "vacuum",
          "form": "surface",
          "key": "Noei.RevealingFormicAcid.2026",
          "wn": 3640,
          "measured_on": "fe3o4_001",
          "technique": "irras",
          "tags": ["direct-dosing"],
          "note": "Fig. 7 region and the accompanying text (IRRAS, UHV, room temperature): O-H stretching modes found around 3640 cm⁻¹ on both magnetite facets. The polarization gives the tilt: roughly 45° from the surface normal for surface hydroxyls and about 75° for the O-H of molecularly adsorbed formic acid. These hydroxyls are where the hydrogen released by the dissociating acid ends up. The paper cautions that O-H bands on magnetite are intrinsically very weak, so the calculated positions could not be checked against experiment."
        },
        {
          "id": 335,
          "key": "Paredes-Nunez.SpectrumBaselineArtefacts.2015",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "wn": 3735,
          "measured_on": "sio2",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 1 (commercial silica, 312 m²/g, annealed in flowing Ar at 350 °C for 5 min, then cooled to 50 °C in 50 °C steps, background the SiC-filled cell): free silanols. Beside them a larger band from 3800 to 3000 cm⁻¹ of hydrogen-bonded silanols, which grows slightly on cooling, most likely as traces of water readsorb."
        },
        {
          "id": 336,
          "key": "Paredes-Nunez.SpectrumBaselineArtefacts.2015",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "surface",
          "wn": [3743, 3445],
          "measured_on": "sio2",
          "technique": "drifts",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. 2 (the same silica at 50, 150 and 250 °C in Ar, referenced to its own spectrum at 350 °C): new free silanols at 3743 cm⁻¹ and hydrogen-bonded hydroxyls around 3445 cm⁻¹, both clearer the lower the temperature, as the surface partly rehydrates. The same difference spectra grow spurious bands in the carbonyl region (see lattice overtones SiO₂)."
        }
      ],
      "tags": []
    },

    {
      "id": "od_stretch",
      "species": "hydroxyl",
      "phase": "surface",
      "group": "support_oh",
