      "atoms": "M-O",
      "wn_start": 200,
      "wn_end": 700,
      "short": "lattice Ga₂O₃",
      "description": "Not an adsorbate. The Raman fingerprint of the gallium oxide support itself, the vibrations of its own Ga-O framework rather than anything sitting on it. It is here for the same reason as the silica overtones: bands on the chart that no adsorbate made. In practice it is a phase check. β-Ga₂O₃ gives a set of sharp lines between about 200 and 700 cm⁻¹ whose pattern identifies the polymorph, which is what a paper means when it says the structure was confirmed by Raman. The infrared sees the same framework, but as absorption strong enough to bury anything adsorbed, so this window belongs to Raman in practice.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 353,
          "key": "Feng.GenerationSurfaceGaH.2024",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "lattice",
          "wn": [
            213,
            283,
            325,
            426,
            573,
            685
          ],
          "measured_on": "ga2o3",
          "technique": "raman",
          "laser_nm": 320,
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Fig. S2 (as-prepared precipitated Ga₂O₃, home-assembled Raman spectrograph, 320 nm excitation): six lines at 213, 283, 325, 426, 573 and 685 cm⁻¹, read from the figure's own labels. The paper assigns none of them individually. Raman is used only alongside XRD (Fig. S1) to confirm the structure of the precipitated solid before any H₂ treatment, so what is recorded here is the pattern as a whole rather than six mode attributions. It is worth carrying because it fixes the identity of the support whose hydroxyls and Ga-H hydrides appear elsewhere in the atlas, measured on this same material. The paper does not state the atmosphere; an ex-situ spectrum of the as-prepared powder is taken in air, which is this collection’s assumption."
        }
