      "short": "ν(In-O-In) c-In₂O₃",
      "description": "The stretch of the In-O-In bridges of cubic bixbyite In₂O₃. It grows as oxygen vacancies form, by breaking one of the double oxygen bridges, so its intensity against the InO₆ bend near 306 cm⁻¹ is the usual Raman measure of how reduced the oxide is; a metal that spills hydrogen onto it, Pd or Re, raises it.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 497,
          "key": "Shen.CO2HydrogenationMethanol.2022",
          "wn": 365,
          "measured_on": "in2o3_c",
          "technique": "raman",
          "laser_nm": 532,
          "form": "lattice",
          "note": "Fig. 3c and Section 3.2 (visible Raman, 532 nm, 7 mW, 10 s, room temperature; cubic bixbyite In₂O₃ precipitated with ammonia and calcined at 723 K, PDF 06-0416), measured after H₂/N₂ (1/9) at 573 K and after 3 h of CO₂ hydrogenation at 573 K: 365 cm⁻¹, the In-O-In stretch, which the paper calls sensitive to oxygen vacancies. As I₂ against I₁ at 306 cm⁻¹ it gives 0.69 for In₂O₃ after reduction and 1.15 after reaction, and 1.14 and 1.22 for 1Re/In₂O₃ and 5Re/In₂O₃ after reduction (Fig. 3e), read as more vacancies with Re and over-reduction of bare In₂O₃ under reaction.",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
