    {
      "id": "silica_boson_peak",
      "species": "silica",
      "phase": "solid",
      "group": "silica",
      "vibration": {
        "category": "lattice",
        "subtype": null,
        "branch": null
      },
      "atoms": "Si-O",
      "wn_start": 45,
      "wn_end": 85,
      "short": "boson peak SiO₂ glass",
      "description": "The boson peak, a broad low-frequency Raman band that is a signature of tetrahedrally connected glasses and has no counterpart in the crystal. In silica glass it lies near 50-60 cm⁻¹; distorting the tetrahedra, as light network modifiers do, raises it. Its origin is still debated, the usual reading being rotation of nearly rigid tetrahedra.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 535,
          "key": "Li.FiberglassScienceTechnology.2021",
          "form": "lattice",
          "wn": 54,
          "measured_on": "sio2_glass",
          "technique": "raman",
          "note": "Ch. 2.1 (Neuville, Cicconi, Blanc and Lancry, Raman spectroscopy of glass and glass fibre), p. 111 and Fig. 2.13: the boson peak of SiO₂ glass at 54 cm⁻¹, moving to 70 cm⁻¹ with 40 mol% Na₂O; heavy modifiers (K, Ba, Sr, Cs) leave it near 50 cm⁻¹, as in silica glass. Ascribed to rotational motion of almost rigid tetrahedra, an origin the chapter calls controversial; it puts the peak near 80 cm⁻¹ in the historical account and fits it near 60 cm⁻¹ in the fibre work.",
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
      "tags": []
    },
    {
      "id": "silica_r_band",
      "species": "silica",
      "phase": "solid",
      "group": "silica",
      "vibration": {
        "category": "lattice",
        "subtype": null,
        "branch": null
      },
      "atoms": "Si-O",
      "wn_start": 400,
      "wn_end": 455,
      "short": "R band (Si-O-Si) SiO₂",
      "description": "The strongest Raman feature of silica glass, the broad R band near 440 cm⁻¹, spread over roughly 410 to 450 cm⁻¹ by the distribution of Si-O-Si angles. It is the motion of the bridging oxygens in the larger rings (five-membered and up), read as a symmetric stretch by some sources and as a bend by others; all agree the oxygen moves. It is the usual internal standard a silica spectrum is normalised to.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 515,
          "key": "Lee.SituRamanSpectroscopy.2008",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "lattice",
          "measured_on": "sio2_fumed",
          "technique": "raman",
          "laser_nm": 532,
          "note": "Section 3.2 and Fig. 2 (in-situ Raman, 532 nm, 10 mW; dehydrated 8% V₂O₅/SiO₂ on the same fumed silica at 450 °C, during H₂¹⁸O exchange), a band the paper attributes to the silica support: the network bending mode, given as a range, 410-450 cm⁻¹, and among the bulk vibrations that do not shift during ¹⁸O exchange.",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "id": 516,
          "key": "Ivanda.RamanSpectroscopySiO2.2003",
          "form": "lattice",
          "wn": 440,
          "measured_on": "sio2_glass",
          "technique": "raman",
          "note": "Fig. 1 and Section 2 (polarised VV Raman, double monochromator with photomultiplier; silica glasses sintered from Degussa OX50 fumed silica, about 120 nm particles, 50 m² g⁻¹; spectra normalised to the amplitude at 440 cm⁻¹): 440 cm⁻¹ is the amplitude every spectrum is normalised to, the main band of the network; the paper names no assignment for it.",
          "tags": []
        },
        {
          "id": 530,
          "key": "Li.FiberglassScienceTechnology.2021",
          "form": "lattice",
          "wn": 440,
          "measured_on": "sio2_glass",
          "technique": "raman",
          "note": "Ch. 2.1 (Neuville, Cicconi, Blanc and Lancry, Raman spectroscopy of glass and glass fibre), pp. 107, 113 and 117: in SiO₂ glass, the band at about 435-440 cm⁻¹, called R in the fibre work, is the symmetric stretch of bridging oxygens, the predominant motion of oxygen in Si-O-Si bonds of five-, six- or higher-membered rings. The chapter adds that whether the motion is a stretch or a bend seems uncertain, but every study points to the oxygen of the Si-O-Si linkage.",
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
      "tags": []
    },
    {
