    {
      "id": "n2_stretch_q",
      "species": "n2",
      "phase": "gas",
      "group": "n2",
      "vibration_modes": ["n2_stretch"],
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "branch": "Q"
      },
      "branch_group": "n2_stretch_branches",
      "atoms": "N-N",
      "wn_start": 2325,
      "wn_end": 2335,
      "short": "ν(NN) N₂",
      "description": "The Q branch of the nitrogen stretch, ΔJ = 0, and effectively the whole of what a Raman spectrum of air shows near 2330 cm⁻¹. N₂ is homonuclear, so the mode is infrared-forbidden and Raman is the only route to it. As a linear molecule in a non-degenerate vibration it takes ΔJ = 0 and ±2 only, so the band carries O, Q and S branches and no P or R. The Q lines pile up at the centre and dominate; the O and S wings hold the rest.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 354,
          "key": "Kojima.QuantitativeAnalysisSpectral.2005",
          "wn": 2331,
          "technique": "srs",
          "laser_nm": 532,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "free",
          "note": "Sect. 3.2 and Fig. 5 (200-shot averaged, 0.1 mm slit, apparatus width 35 cm⁻¹): 2331 cm⁻¹ is the fundamental vibrational Raman frequency the paper assigns to ν₀ for its N₂ line-shape diagram, and the cross-talk discussion places the Q branch near 2330 cm⁻¹. Recorded in room-temperature air at 10 atm to fix the polarizability ratio (a₀/b₀)² = 0.25 for nitrogen, then checked against a 10 atm H₂-air flame at 1425 K. In the paper’s 30 atm spectra, which are simulated rather than measured, the pure-rotational H₂ S(10) line falls completely on top of this branch. The paper names only a pulsed Nd:YAG and never states the line; 532 nm is inferred from its own figure axes.",
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "vs",
      "width": "sharp",
      "confidence": "confirmed",
      "tags": ["ir-inactive"]
    },
    {
      "id": "n2_stretch_o",
      "species": "n2",
      "phase": "gas",
      "group": "n2",
      "vibration_modes": ["n2_stretch"],
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "branch": "O"
      },
      "branch_group": "n2_stretch_branches",
      "atoms": "N-N",
      "wn_start": 2240,
      "wn_end": 2328,
      "short": "ν(NN) N₂",
      "description": "The O branch of the nitrogen stretch, ΔJ = −2, falling below the Q branch. With the S branch above it this is the whole rotational structure a linear molecule in a non-degenerate vibration is allowed, ΔJ = 0 and ±2, which is why nitrogen shows no P or R branch in Raman. It is far weaker than the Q branch and usually reads as a tail rather than as resolved lines. The window is the envelope the populated levels reach, not a reported position.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 355,
          "key": "Kojima.QuantitativeAnalysisSpectral.2005",
          "technique": "srs",
          "laser_nm": 532,
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "free",
          "note": "Fig. 5 and Fig. 8: the O branch of vibrational N₂, marked on the figure as the blue-side tail of the Q branch and resolved in room-temperature air at 10 atm, where its intensity relative to the Q branch fixes the polarizability ratio (a₀/b₀)² = 0.25. The paper prints no position for it, so none is recorded. In the simulated 30 atm spectra it runs into the pure-rotational H₂ S(8) and S(9) lines, overlapping by 48% at an equivalence ratio of 1.5 and by 65% for S(9). Spontaneous Raman with a pulsed Nd:YAG. The paper never states the line: 532 nm is inferred from its own figure axes, where the 2331 cm⁻¹ N₂ Q branch falls at about 607 nm.",
          "tags": []
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "w",
      "width": "broad",
      "confidence": "likely",
      "tags": ["ir-inactive"]
    },
    {
      "id": "n2_stretch_s",
      "species": "n2",
      "phase": "gas",
      "group": "n2",
      "vibration_modes": ["n2_stretch"],
      "vibration": {
