    {
      "id": "carbonic_acid_co_stretch",
      "species": "carbonic_acid",
      "phase": "adsorbed",
      "group": "carbonyl",
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch"
      },
      "atoms": "C=O",
      "wn_start": 1665,
      "wn_end": 1700,
      "short": "ν(C=O) H₂CO₃*",
      "description": "Carbonic acid held on hydroxylated silica, in equilibrium with adsorbed CO₂ and water rather than existing on its own. Free H₂CO₃ is unstable, but multiple hydrogen bonds to surface silanols stabilise it enough to be seen. The band is a trap: monodentate κ¹-formate is calculated to absorb at the same position, and only its lifetime rules it out. It rises and falls with the CO₂ band near 2341 cm⁻¹, and unlike formate it has no partner in the C-H region, which is the positive evidence that no C-H bond is present.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 376,
          "key": "Corral-Perez.DecisiveRolePerimeter.2018",
          "wn": 1688,
          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "measured_on": ["sio2", "ag_sio2"],
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling"],
          "note": "Figs. 4 and S10 (transient DRIFTS, 1 wt % Ag/SiO₂ and bare SiO₂, CO₂:H₂ = 1:1 against Ar, 230 °C, 5 bar), resolved by multivariate curve resolution. Isotope substitution (Fig. S13) is what fixes it: the band moves to 1638 cm⁻¹ under ¹³CO₂ but only to 1684 under D₂, so it is a C-O vibration with almost no hydrogen displacement. It tracks the 2341 cm⁻¹ CO₂ band exactly. Formates, carbonates and formic acid are excluded by the absence of any matching C-H band; κ¹-formate is excluded instead by its calculated lifetime, which on Ag is of the order of a molecular vibration (Table S6)."
        },
        {
          "id": 377,
          "key": "Corral-Perez.DecisiveRolePerimeter.2018",
          "medium": "vacuum",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "wn": 1669,
          "technique": "computational",
          "tags": [],
          "note": "Table S5 (PBE-D″/plane-wave, PHONON package): ν₅, the C=O stretch of H₂CO₃ adsorbed as O(top)+H(bridge) on Ag(111), calculated at 1669 cm⁻¹ against the 1688 cm⁻¹ observed. The same table puts free H₂CO₃ at 1748 cm⁻¹, so adsorption is calculated to pull the stretch down by about 80 cm⁻¹. A caveat on the comparison: the frequency is computed on the metal, while the band is assigned to carbonic acid on the silica, whose stabilisation the paper treats separately in Table S3. A periodic Ag(111) slab in vacuum."
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "m",
      "width": "medium",
      "confidence": "likely",
      "tags": ["misassignment-warning"]
    },
    {
      "id": "co2_silanol_asym",
      "species": "co2",
      "phase": "adsorbed",
      "group": "co2",
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "subtype": "asymmetric"
      },
      "atoms": "O=C=O",
      "wn_start": 2330,
      "wn_end": 2350,
      "short": "νₐₛ(OCO) CO₂*",
      "description": "CO₂ held on silica through its silanol groups, rather than bound to a cation or bent into a carbonate. The position is barely shifted from the free molecule, which is the difficulty: it sits underneath the gas-phase band and is separable only by how it behaves in time, not by where it lies. The silanol stretches lose intensity reversibly as it appears, which is the evidence that the OH groups are what hold it.",
      "based_on": [],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 378,
          "key": "Corral-Perez.DecisiveRolePerimeter.2018",
          "wn": 2341,
          "technique": "drifts",
          "medium": "gas",
          "form": "adsorbed",
          "measured_on": ["sio2", "ag_sio2"],
          "tags": ["isotope-labeling"],
          "note": "Figs. S9, S10 and S12 (transient DRIFTS, 1 wt % Ag/SiO₂ and bare SiO₂, 230 °C, 5 bar). The band lies under gaseous CO₂ and is recovered only because it follows a different time course, separated by multivariate curve resolution in a plug-flow cell with minimal gas volume. Isotope substitution (Fig. S13) moves it to 2276 cm⁻¹ under ¹³CO₂ and leaves it at 2341 under D₂. It appears on bare silica as well as on Ag/SiO₂, and the silanol bands between 3500 and 3800 cm⁻¹ fall reversibly as it grows (Fig. S11), which is what ties it to the OH groups."
        }
      ],
      "intensity": "m",
      "width": "sharp",
      "confidence": "likely",
      "tags": ["misassignment-warning"]
    },
    {
      "id": "co2_aq_asym",
      "species": "co2",
      "phase": "solution",
      "group": "co2",
      "vibration_modes": [
        "co2_stretch_asymmetric"
      ],
      "vibration": {
        "category": "stretch",
        "subtype": "asymmetric",
        "branch": null
      },
      "atoms": "O=C=O",
      "wn_start": 2335,
      "wn_end": 2345,
      "short": "νₐₛ(OCO) CO₂(aq)",
      "description": "Asymmetric stretch of CO₂ dissolved in a liquid. In solution the molecule no longer rotates freely, so the P and R envelopes of the gas collapse into a single band a little below the gas origin at 2349 cm⁻¹. In electrochemical difference spectra taken during CO₂ reduction it usually points down: the electrode consumes CO₂ from the thin layer of electrolyte the beam samples, so the band reports interfacial depletion, not an adsorbate. Gas-phase CO₂ in the optical path sits at nearly the same place and is told apart by its rotational envelope.",
      "based_on": [],
      "confidence": "likely",
      "tags": [
        "raman-inactive"
      ],
      "references": [
        {
          "id": 400,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "wn": 2340,
          "technique": "seiras",
          "medium": "liquid",
          "form": "dissolved",
          "note": "Fig. 2a to c: Ag film electrode in CO₂-saturated 0.1 M CsHCO₃, static and pulsed electrolysis alike. A downward band near 2340 cm⁻¹ marks dissolved CO₂ consumed from the electrolyte next to the electrode; the spectra are differences against a reference spectrum, so down means less. No surface is recorded because the molecule is in the electrolyte, not on the silver. The paper’s diffusion model and mass spectrometry say the same from the other side: interfacial CO₂ is replenished during the anodic pulse."
        },
        {
          "id": 407,
          "key": "Ye.MolecularLevelInsights.2024",
          "wn": 2345,
          "technique": "seiras",
          "medium": "liquid",
          "form": "dissolved",
